.\" mutos_c1.1 - manual page for the MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler back end
.\" Formatted with the standard man(7) macro set (.TH/.SH/.TP/...).
.\" View with: man ./mutos_c1.1   or   groff -man -Tascii mutos_c1.1 | less
.TH MUTOS_C1 1 "September 2026" "MUTOS 1700" "MUTOS 1700 Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mutos_c1 \- MUTOS 1700 cross C compiler, second pass (code generator)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutos_c1
.I temp1
.I temp2
.I assembly.s
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_c1
is the second of the two passes that make up the MUTOS 1700 cross C
compiler (the first is
.BR mutos_c0 (1)).
It reads the tagged intermediate-code stream written to
.I temp1
/
.I temp2
by
.BR mutos_c0 (1)
and writes
.BR mutos_as (1)-syntax
assembly text to
.IR assembly.s .
.PP
This is a from-scratch, modern-C11 reimplementation, byte-for-byte
verified against real MUTOS 1700 hardware output, not a literal port
of the original Version 7 Unix
.BR cc (1) 's
second pass; see
.I src/mutos_cc/README.md
in the toolchain source tree for the confirmed
.IR temp1 / temp2
wire format and the exact opcode coverage this pass currently
implements.
.SH "CURRENT SCOPE"
.B mutos_c1
implements exactly the opcodes
.BR mutos_c0 (1) 's
current grammar scope can produce:
.BR SYMDEF ,
.BR PROG ,
.BR EVEN ,
.BR RLABEL ,
.BR SAVE ,
.BR SETREG ,
.BR BRANCH ,
.BR LABEL ,
.BR ANAME ,
.BR NAME ,
.BR CON ,
.BR PLUS ,
.BR MINUS ,
.BR TIMES ,
.BR DIVIDE ,
.BR MOD ,
.BR ASSIGN ,
.BR RFORCE ,
.BR EXPR ,
.BR RETRN ,
.BR SETSTK ,
and
.BR EOFC .
Every compiled function gets the fixed, unconditional MUTOS 1700 C
calling-convention prologue/epilogue (see
.I docs/MUTOS_C_ABI.md
in the toolchain source tree): \c
.BR "push\ bp" ,
.BR "mov\ bp,sp" ,
.BR "push\ di" ,
.BR "push\ si" ,
a body, and a tail jump to the shared
.B cret
epilogue routine. A local-variable frame
.RI ( SETSTK
value beyond the fixed 4-byte register-save area) of up to 76 bytes
emits a plain
.BR "sub sp,N" ;
above 256 bytes it emits
.BR "mov ax,N / call chkstk" ;
the gap in between is left as an explicit "not yet supported"
diagnostic rather than a guess, since the real compiler's exact
.B "sub sp,N"
-versus-
.B "call chkstk"
threshold is only bounded, not pinned down exactly, by the evidence
gathered so far (see
.I docs/MUTOS_C_ABI.md
sect. 1.9). Binary-operator code generation (\c
.BR PLUS ", " MINUS ", " TIMES ", " DIVIDE ", " MOD )
currently only covers a single level of constant/variable operands
each (matching exactly what
.BR mutos_c0 (1) 's
grammar can produce); an immediate right-hand operand to
.BR TIMES / DIVIDE / MOD
(the 8086's single-operand
.BR IMUL / IDIV
takes a register or memory operand, never an immediate directly) and
direct memory-to-memory
.B ASSIGN
are likewise explicit "not yet supported" cases.
Any
.I temp1
opcode outside the list above is reported, never silently
misassembled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I temp1
Input path for the primary intermediate-code stream, as written by
.BR mutos_c0 (1).
.TP
.I temp2
Input path for the string-literal side stream (read for command-line
compatibility; not yet consumed by any opcode this pass implements).
.TP
.I assembly.s
Output path for the generated
.BR mutos_as (1)-syntax
assembly text.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A malformed or unsupported
.I temp1
/
.I temp2
stream (an unrecognized opcode, a missing tag-byte marker, a
truncated stream, or an opcode this pass does not yet implement) is
reported to standard error and causes a non-zero exit status
immediately \(en
.B mutos_c1
does not attempt to produce partial or best-effort assembly output
for input it cannot fully handle.
.SH BUGS
.B mutos_cc,
the driver that would normally chain
.BR mutos_cpp (1),
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_c1 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
and
.BR mutos_ld (1)
together into a single pipeline, does not exist yet; invoke this pass
directly (see
.BR SYNOPSIS ),
or see
.I tests/mutos_cc/run_goldens.sh
for a scripted example.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mutos_c0 (1),
.BR mutos_as (1),
.BR mutos_ld (1)
